Laboratory Manager and Experimental Researcher
United Kingdom

UE07:&nbsp;£41,885.00&nbsp;to&nbsp;£49,798.00&nbsp;Per Annum.

College of Science and Engineering / School of GeoSciences.

Full Time - 35 hours per week.

Fixed Term Contract - 24 months.

&nbsp;

The Opportunity:&nbsp;

The University of Edinburgh is a world-class, research-intensive institution. We offer a supportive working environment, excellent facilities, and opportunities for professional development within a diverse and international community.&nbsp;

We are seeking a Laboratory Manager and Experimental Researcher to support hands-on experimental research and the routine day-to-day operation of the GeoTRON laboratory within the School of GeoSciences. The laboratory supports research in natural hydrogen, CO

₂

 storage, hydrogen storage, geoenergy and wider subsurface energy systems.&nbsp;

You will take primary day-to-day responsibility for preparing, conducting, monitoring and documenting high-pressure/high-temperature (HPHT) rock–fluid–gas experiments in accordance with agreed protocols and safety procedures. This includes sample preparation and tracking, pressure and temperature checks, gas and fluid sampling, real-time gas monitoring, routine troubleshooting and coordination of analytical workflows.&nbsp;

Alongside experimental delivery, you will coordinate laboratory readiness, equipment and consumables, gas cylinders, maintenance records, approved-user access and local inductions. You will maintain clear QA/QC documentation and traceable experimental data, and contribute technical and scientific material to reports, presentations and publications. You will work closely with researchers and technical, analytical and safety teams, under the direction of the line manager, who sets overall priorities and approves significant changes and commitments.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Your skills and attributes for success:&nbsp;&nbsp;

An MSc/Master’s degree, or equivalent professional experience, in geoscience, chemistry, chemical engineering, materials science, environmental science, geoenergy or a closely related field.&nbsp;

Significant hands-on experience in a research, technical or industrial laboratory, including planning, conducting and documenting experiments within agreed protocols.&nbsp;

Experience preparing and handling rock, fluid, gas, chemical or material samples, and the ability to coordinate relevant analytical work (such as gas analysis/GC, ICP, XRD, SEM/EDS or microscopy).&nbsp;

Experience with routine laboratory organisation, including equipment bookings, consumables, compressed gases/cylinders, sample inventories, maintenance logs and laboratory records.&nbsp;

A sound understanding of laboratory health and safety, risk assessments, SOPs, safe handling of equipment and compressed gases, and QA/QC requirements.&nbsp;

Ability to identify and troubleshoot routine equipment, calibration, leakage, contamination and sample-tracking issues, and maintain clear, reproducible experimental records.&nbsp;

Excellent organisation, communication, reliability and attention to detail; ability to work independently within agreed procedures and priorities and to support other laboratory users.&nbsp;

Experience with HPHT systems, reactors, rock–fluid–gas experiments, gas monitoring or relevant subsurface energy research would be advantageous, but is not essential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

A PhD (completed or near completion) in geochemistry, geoenergy, chemistry, chemical engineering, or a closely related discipline would be an advantage, but is not essential.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Working pattern:
&nbsp;

This is a fully on-site, laboratory-based role in Edinburgh, undertaken during agreed working hours. Owing to the need for routine monitoring of equipment, gases, samples and laboratory readiness, home working, compressed hours and other flexible working patterns are not expected to be compatible with this position.&nbsp;

Informal enquiries may be directed to Dr Aliakbar Hassanpouryouzband (

Hssnpr@ed.ac.uk

).&nbsp;

Apply to help deliver high-quality experimental research and maintain a safe, reliable and well-organised laboratory supporting subsurface energy research.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle:&nbsp;

• Competitive salary&nbsp;

• Generous annual leave and public holiday entitlement&nbsp;

• Defined benefits pension scheme&nbsp;

• A wide range of staff discounts&nbsp;

• Family-friendly initiatives and wellbeing support&nbsp;

• Professional development and training opportunities&nbsp;

&nbsp;

This post is full-time/ (35 hours per week)

